course: dictionary
lesson: 1930
title: The comma
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A comma is a small mark (,) that tells the reader to pause for a moment, or to separate parts of a sentence. Look at «eggs, milk and bread». The commas stop the words running together, so the reader knows there are three things to buy.
explain: Commas also help at the start of a sentence. «After lunch, we played outside.» The comma comes after the first part, because that part tells us when. Read the sentence without the comma and you will feel the pause is missing.
explain: Commas can also wrap extra information. «My brother, who is ten, plays chess.» The words between the commas add an extra detail. Take them out and the sentence still makes sense. Test it yourself: can you remove the bit inside the commas and still read a full sentence?
fact: A comma is a punctuation mark: ,
fact: Commas separate items in a list, as in «eggs, milk and bread».
fact: A comma after the start of a sentence shows where the first part ends, as in «After lunch, we played».
fact: In big numbers, commas group digits in threes, as in «1,000,000».
fact: In many European countries, a comma is used as the decimal point instead, so 2,5 means two and a half.
exercise: Which line correctly separates the items in a list? | apples, pears, and, plums | apples pears and plums | *apples, pears and plums | apples pears, and plums
exercise: In «After lunch, we played outside», what does the comma show? | A number in a sum | *The end of the first part, before the main sentence | A full stop | A question is coming
exercise: What is the comma doing in the number «1,000,000»? | Starting a list | *Grouping the digits in threes | Showing a decimal point | Asking a question
test: In «My brother, who is ten, plays chess», which words are extra information between the commas? | *who is ten | plays chess | My brother plays | chess
test: In many European countries, what does a comma do in a number like 4,5? | It ends the sentence | *It is the decimal point, so 4,5 means four and a half | It groups thousands, like 4,500 | It shows a question
exam: Which sentence uses a comma correctly after the start of the sentence? | Later that day it, snowed. | Later that, day it snowed | Later that day it snowed, | *Later that day, it snowed.
